 ### 14.1 Bluetooth MIDI (SMK-37 only, roadmap step 5)
 
-BLE-01. Bluetooth MIDI is BLE MIDI only (no Bluetooth audio). It needs a controller image in the Bluetooth block (FLS-13). The user builds the image on their own computer from JieLi's files (ble/ctl/build_ctl.sh, pinned gitee commit d179b44, btctrler.a SHA-256 9ef42624...). X0X never ships the image or any JieLi Bluetooth file. The image is a separate program: the app talks to it only through standard HCI packets in a shared-RAM mailbox. [USER] [CODE ../hal/x0x_ble_abi.h] [CODE ble.c]
-BLE-02. Without an image, or with an image that fails its check, the app starts exactly as before: core 1 starts with its own stacks, no controller code runs, no interrupt is routed to core 1. GLO shows NO IMAGE or BAD IMAGE. The check covers the header magic and version, the ABI, the size, the CRC-32, the init and poll entries (inside the image), the RAM window (0x01C01800-0x01C07EFF) and the interrupts (none that X0X uses on core 0). [USER] [CODE ble/ble_glue.h ble_img_check] [CODE ble.c ble_prepare]
-BLE-03. The controller starts only at power-on, and only when BLUETOOTH is ON. Its init runs once on core 0 at power-on, before core 1 starts and before the audio starts (about 0.6 s; the watchdog is fed before and after). Core 1 then runs the controller: its interrupts and its poll. The RF init gets a fixed crystal trim of 11/11 (0x0B, the SDK default and the value of the working SMK-37 Oscuro firmware). Before a67 it got the trim that the boot left in WLA_CON12. The controller's interrupts stay off during the init. Its first poll on core 1 routes them to core 1 again and turns them on. While the controller runs, the core-0 guard for accesses to invalid bus addresses is off (the RF calibration of the JieLi files touches the radio registers); the stack, write and PC guards stay on. While core 1 runs the controller, core 0's stacks are closed to writes from core 1 and from DMA (write limit 3). The BLE TX level is 6, as in the working SMK-37 Oscuro firmware (11 before a66; level 5 did not change the core 0 failure of test 20). While the timer 5 interrupt runs, its 8 saved words on core 0's stack are closed to writes from core 0, core 1 and DMA (write limit 4) and checked at 4 points; a change is recorded and set back. Core 1 watches core 0's ms counter: when it stops for 100 ms, core 1 records core 0's last branches (ETM), DBG_MSG and EMU_MSG. Core 1 also checks 8 KiB of unused RAM (0x01C72000-0x01C74000, set to all ones before the start) and records changed words. Before the RF init, with the interrupts off and core 1 not started, X0X sets the stock supply: DCDC13 on and LDO13 off, VDC14 1.60 V, VDDIOM and VDDIOW 3.2 V, SYSVDD 1.38 V (the stock V15 order). The rails and P3_ANA_CON0 before and after are recorded (DIAG). With BLUETOOTH OFF the boot supply does not change. The system clock stays 360 MHz. The controller gets the host's HCI frames through the entry it registers with its transport (hci_h4_download_data), not through the library's H4 send. Then core 1 calls its poll entry between audio jobs. If the start fails, or the image reports a failure later, X0X stops calling it, turns its interrupts off on core 1 and shows FAILED. If its start does not end in time, core 1 is held and X0X runs on one core. [CODE ble.c ble_start] [CODE ../hal/fm1_cpu1.h] (Changed 2026-10-15: 4 s, the bus guard, the stack write limit, the HCI entry, TX level 11 again, the frame watch, core 1's watch of core 0 and the RAM canary, the stock supply setup. Changed 2026-10-16: TX level 6, crystal trim 11/11.)
-BLE-04. Before X0X turns the flash off (erase, write, SPI read), it parks core 1: core 1 waits in RAM with its interrupts off and runs no controller code. If core 1 does not park within 20 ms, X0X holds it (one core, no Bluetooth). Without a controller nothing is parked. [CODE ../hal/fm1_cpu1.h fm1_cpu1_park] [CODE ../hal/fm1_flash.h]
-BLE-05. GLO > BLUETOOTH MIDI has three rows. BLUETOOTH OFF/ON (default OFF, saved in the settings byte that was reserved; an older project loads OFF). STATUS (read only): NO IMAGE, OFF, RESTART, STARTING, READY (advertising), CONNECTED, FAILED or BAD IMAGE. NAME (read only): "X0X-" and 4 hex digits. ON when the controller did not start at power-on shows "RESTART TO TURN ON". OFF while it runs drops the connection and stops advertising. [CODE app/ui.c] [CODE app/x0x.h settings_t.bt]
-BLE-06. The unit advertises as a BLE MIDI peripheral with a random static address. The address and the name come from a CRC of the factory record (USRTRIM, read only), so they stay the same for a unit across power cycles and differ between units. One connection at a time. [CODE ble/ble_glue.h ble_addr_of] [CODE ble/ble_midi_host.c]
-BLE-07a. X0X sends no connection parameter request (tests 31 and 34 made the iPhone link drop or slow down). DIAG shows the interval in use, the MTU the central asked for, the pairing refusals, the rejected L2CAP requests, the last link's end reason and the MIDI in drops. [CODE ble.c ble_h_link0] (Changed 2026-10-15, test 37.)
-BLE-07. No pairing or bonding. A pairing request gets "Pairing Not Supported". Nothing is written to BTIF (0xC0000). [CODE ble/ble_midi_host.c smp_in]
-BLE-08. BLE MIDI in acts like USB MIDI in: notes, clock, Start, Stop, Continue go into the same input queue, and notes first go through the same APC40 clip and transport feedback handling (clip colours on the pads). The BLE timestamps are not used for scheduling. Up to 64 incoming messages wait between two service ticks; more are dropped (counted). Incoming SysEx: a Universal Device Inquiry and the APC40 mkII introduction get the same replies as on USB, sent over Bluetooth (Loopy Pro can use the BLE port as its APC40). Other SysEx is not used (it is counted). LIM-10 applies: clock from USB and Bluetooth together counts twice. [CODE ble.c ble_service ble_sysex_in] (Changed 2026-10-15: APC40 over Bluetooth.)
-BLE-09. Everything X0X sends as MIDI out (clock, Start, Stop, notes) also goes to a connected BLE MIDI central that has notifications on. The APC40 identity and introduction replies to a SysEx that came over Bluetooth go to Bluetooth (BLE-08). In clip mode, the APC feedback replies, the pad clip-launch notes and the keybed notes (CLP-60) go to USB and to a connected BLE MIDI central, so Loopy Pro on the BLE port can launch clips and get the keys. (Changed 2026-10-16: the keybed notes.) The undo/redo history notes stay on USB only. The PLAY note of MID-14 goes to USB and to Bluetooth (added 2026-10-16, a74). (Changed 2026-10-15.) SysEx is not mirrored. [CODE app/plat_fm1.c plat_midi_out] [CODE app/main_fm1.c]
-BLE-10. Every install writes the whole app slot, also the Bluetooth block. To keep Bluetooth, add the image again for each update: tools/add_ctl.py, tools/fm1_install.py --ctl FILE (it also uses ~/.config/x0x/ctl.img by itself; --no-ctl skips it), or the web installer's ctl.img field (SMK-37 only). Without it the block is empty after the install (BLE-02). [CODE ../../tools/add_ctl.py] [CODE ../../tools/fm1_install.py] [CODE ../../web/fm1pkg.js insertCtl]
-BLE-11. tools/add_ctl.py and the web installer write the Bluetooth block only when the block is free: all 0xFF, or a controller image (X0BC header), or the package has the slot map record (FLS-14, the build limits the app code since then). An older X0X package (for example 1.8-apc40) has app code in the block. They refuse it with a message, also for --remove. tools/fm1_install.py --ctl FILE refuses it too (exit badpkg). The cached ~/.config/x0x/ctl.img never stops an install: on such a package the installer skips it with a warning and installs the package without the image. (Added 2026-10-15.) [CODE ../../tools/add_ctl.py block_free] [CODE ../../tools/fm1_install.py with_ctl] [CODE ../../web/fm1pkg.js insertCtl]
-BLE-12. A start record in RAM that a reset keeps (.noinit) protects the power-on. Before the init the app marks the start as trying. A start that does not end READY (not in time, wrong job, not ready), and a power-on that finds the mark still set (the unit crashed, hung or was turned off during the start or in the first 10 s after READY), blocks Bluetooth: the controller is not started at the next power-ons, and STATUS shows FAILED while BLUETOOTH stays ON. So a failing start ends in FAILED, not in a crash at each power-on. 10 s after READY the start counts as good and the mark is cleared. Set BLUETOOTH OFF to clear the block (STATUS OFF); ON again then starts the controller at the next power-on. Turn the unit off only when READY has shown for 10 s. (Added 2026-10-15.) [CODE ble/ble_glue.h ble_guard_*] [CODE ble.c ble_prepare ble_service plat_ble_set]
-BLE-13. The editor command 33 DIAG (read only, web/EDITOR_PROTOCOL.md) returns the crash record, the boot guard counts, the start record of BLE-12 and the live Bluetooth state. The image writes its start stage, its time, its delay count, the last delay's callers and stalls of the tick counter to the mailbox (dbg words). After a reset during the start, the start record also keeps the reset source of that reset. If TIMER4 stands still during the start, core 0 does not wait for ever: the start fails (STATUS FAILED). The first 1 KiB of RAM (0x01C00000) holds no code: it is filled before the start and checked after it, so DIAG shows whether the radio wrote there. The check after the start comes before the first P33 access. Core 0's wait records how it ended (result, TIMER4 stalls, time) in RAM that survives a reset. When the image stops on a library reset request or assert, it records the caller and its heap use (peak, failed allocations). The start record also keeps WLA_CON12 (the crystal trim) before the start and after the init. DIAG also gives the HCI rings (written, read), the host's step, the frames the controller took and gave, the last frame from the controller (type, length, first 6 bytes), and core 0's last Bluetooth tick (time, core 1 poll count), and the rails. Editor command BT_TEST op 3 returns the last boot's timer 5 frame watch; op 4 returns core 1's records (core 0 seen stopping, the RAM canary). The mailbox and host values are raw: after a reset they are the last start's. (Added 2026-10-15.) [CODE app/editor.c ED_DIAG] [CODE ble.c ble_diag] [CODE ../../ble/ctl/src/ctl_main.c stage]
-BLE-14. The editor command 34 BT_TEST (SMK-37 with Bluetooth, web/EDITOR_PROTOCOL.md) is for tools/ble_autotest.py. Op 1 clears the start block as BLUETOOTH OFF does; the BLUETOOTH setting does not change. Op 2 restarts the unit 200 ms after the reply (a chip reset, as after a crash). It never starts the update loader. (Added 2026-10-15.) [CODE app/editor.c ED_BT_TEST] [CODE app/main_fm1.c]
-BLE-15. The editor command 38 INLOG (SMK-37, read only, web/EDITOR_PROTOCOL.md) is the inbound recorder. The firmware keeps the last 16 inbound clip messages in a ring: the time, the source (0 USB, 1 Bluetooth, 3 Bluetooth while notify is off, no cell change), the status and the two data bytes. Source 3 is a Bluetooth note for a clip cell while notify is off: the cell did not change (CLP-18). The ring also keeps each change of the Bluetooth link (source 2: connected, notify on). It keeps only note-on and note-off messages for notes 0-98. Argument 0 also returns the 40 clip velocities and channels. Argument 1 returns the 16 pad colours in the pad colour store and the 16 pad colours in the LED driver data. tools/ble_autotest.py --diag-only shows them as text and writes MISMATCH when a pad does not agree with its cell. (Added 2026-10-16, a76.) [CODE app/smk_inlog.h] [CODE app/main_fm1.c smk_inlog_reply] [CODE ../../tools/inlog_fmt.py] [TEST tests/smk_inlog_test.c]
